From: Evil.2000 <evil.2000@web.de>
Subject: [PATCH] xfrm: state: insert SW states behind the packet offload states

Packet offload (HW) states are kept at the head of each hash chain, and
the lookups made for a packet offload policy or SA (xfrm_state_find(),
__xfrm_state_lookup()) stop at the first SW state they meet.

XFRM_STATE_INSERT() breaks this order when a SW state goes into a chain
that holds only HW states: the walk ends with _x == NULL and the SW
state is added at the head, in front of the HW states, which the lookups
for the packet offload policy then no longer reach. XfrmOutNoStates
counts the dropped packets.

The two SAs of a tunnel share a chain of the bydst table (the hash
mixes source and destination). With the outbound SA offloaded and the
inbound SA in software, as on network processors that only offload the
outbound direction, adding the outbound SA first and the inbound one
second leaves the tunnel without an outbound state: nothing is sent.
The other order works.

Remember the last HW state at the head and add a SW state behind it.

Seen on a FortiGate 100E (NP6Lite, outbound packet offload only) on
6.18.55; the same code is in mainline, ipsec and ipsec-next. With
this patch, the outbound SA added first works, the other order still
works, and the inbound SW SA can be removed and added again while the
offloaded SA stays in the chain.

Status: local fix, not submitted upstream yet. Until it is merged, an
outbound packet offload SA must be added after the SW SA of the reverse
direction on kernels without this patch.

---
diff -ruN a/net/xfrm/xfrm_state.c b/net/xfrm/xfrm_state.c
--- a/net/xfrm/xfrm_state.c
+++ b/net/xfrm/xfrm_state.c
@@ -97,21 +97,24 @@
 
 #define XFRM_STATE_INSERT(by, _n, _h, _type)                               \
 	{                                                                  \
-		struct xfrm_state *_x = NULL;                              \
+		struct xfrm_state *_x, *_hw = NULL;                        \
 									   \
+		/* HW SAs are kept at the head of the list, a SW SA goes   \
+		 * behind them: lookups for packet offload stop at the     \
+		 * first SW SA they meet.                                  \
+		 */                                                        \
 		if (_type != XFRM_DEV_OFFLOAD_PACKET) {                    \
 			hlist_for_each_entry_rcu(_x, _h, by) {             \
-				if (_x->xso.type == XFRM_DEV_OFFLOAD_PACKET) \
-					continue;                          \
-				break;                                     \
+				if (_x->xso.type != XFRM_DEV_OFFLOAD_PACKET) \
+					break;                             \
+				_hw = _x;                                  \
 			}                                                  \
 		}                                                          \
 									   \
-		if (!_x || _x->xso.type == XFRM_DEV_OFFLOAD_PACKET)        \
-			/* SAD is empty or consist from HW SAs only */     \
-			hlist_add_head_rcu(_n, _h);                        \
+		if (_hw)                                                   \
+			hlist_add_behind_rcu(_n, &_hw->by);                \
 		else                                                       \
-			hlist_add_before_rcu(_n, &_x->by);                 \
+			hlist_add_head_rcu(_n, _h);                        \
 	}
 
 static void xfrm_hash_transfer(struct hlist_head *list,
